# Phase Two — Sitewide UI Architecture

The Awtsmoos renews every interface while no selector owns itself or any neighboring page;
Awtsmoos.com is remembered as each visual vessel receives one bounded root and one coherent stage.

## Competing strategies

### A — One giant global stylesheet

Rejected. It would make every page look superficially consistent while reintroducing global selector conflicts, accidental inheritance, z-index wars, and fragile regressions.

### B — Fix only pages users report

Rejected. The screenshots prove the defect class is systemic: native controls and incomplete component ownership can hide across routes until manually discovered.

### C — Scoped page roots plus reusable namespaced primitives

Chosen. Each page owns a root namespace. Shared visual primitives may be imported, but their selectors remain namespaced and their DOM contracts explicit.

### D — Replace every native control with JavaScript widgets

Rejected as a blanket rule. Native semantics remain valuable. Text fields/selects/buttons can be fully CSS-owned while preserving semantics. Audio/video require a custom visible controller layered over an accessible media element.

### E — Runtime auto-styler that mutates arbitrary DOM

Rejected as primary architecture. It hides incomplete source ownership and creates mutation-order conflicts. Runtime audit instrumentation may detect defects, but source modules must own presentation.

## Chosen visual architecture

### Page ownership

Every audited page must expose a stable application/page root. Local CSS selectors begin beneath that root. Shared modules expose explicit class contracts and remain scoped.

### Interactive primitive contract

Every relevant interactive surface must define:

- resting appearance;
- fine-pointer hover;
- active/pressed feedback;
- focus-visible outline/ring;
- disabled state;
- invalid state when applicable;
- reduced-motion fallback;
- touch target floor;
- text wrapping/overflow behavior.

### Media contract

Visible native `<audio controls>` / `<video controls>` are audit failures on branded surfaces. Media remains semantic underneath while a custom controller owns play/pause, timeline, time, volume/mute, buffering/error, keyboard focus, and compact/mobile layout.

### Geometry contract

- `max-inline-size: 100%` where content may grow.
- fixed/sticky layers must reserve or explicitly compensate content space.
- drawers/dialogs/menus must use viewport-bounded sizing and internal overflow.
- safe-area insets participate in phone fixed layers.
- no accidental document horizontal overflow.
- no half-visible controls at initial fold boundaries.

### Stacking contract

Each page defines a small named stacking vocabulary: base/content/sticky/popover/dialog/toast or equivalent. Raw arbitrary four-digit z-index escalation is rejected.

### Animation contract

Motion communicates state or hierarchy. Avoid infinite distraction. Hover transformations remain fine-pointer-only. Active feedback remains universal. Reduced-motion removes nonessential displacement while keeping state contrast.

## Audit engine architecture

Create a source/runtime audit that can report route-level findings without auto-mutating pages. Candidate checks:

- HTML pages containing branded native audio/video controls;
- form elements not matched by owning page/style selectors;
- bare/global CSS selectors in local style bundles;
- hard-coded z-index hotspots;
- fixed/sticky declarations;
- missing viewport metadata;
- browser runtime X overflow;
- visible elements outside viewport;
- undersized interactive targets;
- default computed appearance signatures for form/media controls.

## First remediation reference

Social Hub becomes the reference page because the user supplied live screenshots proving:

- native audio chrome inside messages;
- incomplete composer integration;
- fixed-bottom geometry risk;
- mixed visual ownership.

Fix shared Social Hub components first so private messages and social pulse benefit together when they consume the same primitives.
